#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073642 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "35be9962-fbfd-57f5-906e-41660079063d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5171" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/id #uuid "ccefedb4-9e45-56c4-bb11-460ed8fe1793" :question/math-academy-id "e-5172" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $x^3 - x^2 - x = 3x^2 + 4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x & =3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x & =4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x.$ We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4x - 5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = -1,$ and $x = 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "830e79ed-49ac-537f-aa67-9bfd063bb258" :question/math-academy-id "e-5173" :question/problem "If $3x^5 + x^3 = 3x^3 - 5x^4,$ then what are the values of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{4} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x^3.$ We can factor out the $x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3x^2+5x-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = \\dfrac{1}{3},$ and $x = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14dc1c65-04df-5fbd-be61-4477e30ea100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{1}{2},0,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431365} {:answer/id #uuid "21aebf18-ce11-5430-a764-9bb724cacfd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,2,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431369} {:answer/id #uuid "2c17f057-bfa1-5976-b130-cd6d0a8fb8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-2,0,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431372} {:answer/id #uuid "9d08a714-6f3f-5167-98b9-80593532b035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{3}{2},0,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431404} {:answer/id #uuid "f0adf3bb-6907-5333-bed7-5a181b9295d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{1}{2},1,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14dc1c65-04df-5fbd-be61-4477e30ea100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{1}{2},0,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0959ab45-d3bb-507d-9b03-97c7b3eb10dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "598f0d12-0057-595a-88df-823e76f024dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114097" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=8{w}^{3}-20{w}^{2}-12w.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n8{w}^{3}-20{w}^{2}-12w=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4w.$ We can factor out the $4w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w(2{w}^{2}-5w-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{w}^{2}-5w-3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w(2{w}^{2}-5w-3) & =0 \\\\ 4w(2w+1)(w-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $w=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e2c11b-95eb-5f08-a9a0-8be6291b080e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431361} {:answer/id #uuid "17dca348-b860-5d6a-8c49-01c5dabaa699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=0,2,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431366} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6656eb-e900-52d1-80d6-743f4beaedf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{2}{3},0,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431368} {:answer/id #uuid "56d3c364-0918-5bee-aefe-a198ca16efe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-3,-\\frac{2}{3},0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431386} {:answer/id #uuid "8f183aaa-0947-5d53-96df-c8c4236d1225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{2},0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b6656eb-e900-52d1-80d6-743f4beaedf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{2}{3},0,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431368} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db8c159-2034-5a4d-9ed7-a18d7e127cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a572c317-dda1-5da3-ae6b-b774d6ba6280" :question/math-academy-id "q-114108" :question/problem "If $14{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}=2{p}^{4}+17{p}^{2},$ then what are the values of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2} & =2{p}^{4}+17{p}^{2} \\\\ 12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2} & =17{p}^{2} \\\\ 12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}-12{p}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2{p}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $2{p}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{p}^{4}-10{p}^{3}-12{p}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}(6{p}^{2}-5p-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{p}^{2}-5p-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{p}^{2}(6{p}^{2}-5p-6) & =0 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}(3p+2)(2p-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $p=0,$ $p=-\\frac{2}{3},$ and $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e9fedc-4d5e-5788-be94-10e0f727eb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-\\frac{3}{2},0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431362} {:answer/id #uuid "0b30b2b8-aa8f-5c31-b7e3-bc987c2700fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=-2,0,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2e1eb1-9a53-5789-b781-a03ef2aeb0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431414} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b64a4c-411a-532f-abeb-5237ce881e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{5}{3},2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431427} {:answer/id #uuid "f01b3681-4519-57fb-bc76-853ded546956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{4},1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f01b3681-4519-57fb-bc76-853ded546956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "w=0,\\frac{3}{4},1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1caed9b-00a5-576f-9db3-b95772341ed6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af7e750a-c93b-50d4-aa96-76ae41484ef4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114116" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $15{w}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $15{w}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{w}^{2}-7w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0 \\\\ 15{w}^{4}(4w-3)(w-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $w=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01025723-2564-5015-9d6f-9159e6f39f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-3,\\,0,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431358} {:answer/id #uuid "03d8b461-4ae9-52a8-aca8-51e298ef0397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,0,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431360} {:answer/id #uuid "af85b59e-19c9-5c0f-890e-e229261732cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-4,\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431410} {:answer/id #uuid "b2706b10-456e-5cf0-af26-738518efe438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431412} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b19f27-5dfc-57bb-b7ed-81e28ee0fafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=0,\\,4,\\,\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af85b59e-19c9-5c0f-890e-e229261732cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-4,\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431410} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f44f09-12f5-5b2f-83a3-7b68c75a5ed0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9d205c9-f507-5289-895e-3b775edf64c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-13466" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $-6{z}^{3}-12{z}^{2}+20z=3{z}^{2}-16z.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(z)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{z}^{3}-12{z}^{2}+20z & =3{z}^{2}-16z \\\\ -6{z}^{3}-15{z}^{2}+20z & =-16z \\\\ -6{z}^{3}-15{z}^{2}+36z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $-3z.$ We can factor out the $-3z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{z}^{3}-15{z}^{2}+36z & =0 \\\\ -3z(2{z}^{2}+5z-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{z}^{2}+5z-12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3z(2{z}^{2}+5z-12) & =0 \\\\ -3z(2z-3)(z+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $z=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4582def6-f856-5add-b613-0218c9f9fe54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-2,\\,0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431381} {:answer/id #uuid "4e63d264-ea50-58ed-916c-7de30c6c0798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-2,\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431383} {:answer/id #uuid "c08f4492-345f-5b87-8634-052cc34abc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-2,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431417} {:answer/id #uuid "dc40935f-e545-5418-9a22-e109cc113b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,0,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431424} {:answer/id #uuid "f911e318-36c7-56ee-b300-a669a11ca6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=0,\\,2,\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e63d264-ea50-58ed-916c-7de30c6c0798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s=-2,\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431383} :answer-field/id #uuid "971c6c2e-9221-5a95-aafb-62f84bc18983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae27ee23-00a1-5698-9779-e950f4da30dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-14007" :question/problem "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $12{s}^{3}+12{s}^{2}-20s=6{s}^{2}+16s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(s)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{s}^{3}+12{s}^{2}-20s & =6{s}^{2}+16s \\\\ 12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-20s & =16s \\\\ 12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-36s & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $6s.$ We can factor out the $6s$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{s}^{3}+6{s}^{2}-36s & =0 \\\\ 6s(2{s}^{2}+s-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{s}^{2}+s-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6s(2{s}^{2}+s-6) & =0 \\\\ 6s(2s-3)(s+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $s=0,$ $s=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $s=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5059480d-49b5-5e4a-bd13-82ce2b77ee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-6,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431384} {:answer/id #uuid "5713fbbf-27f4-5c95-b6fd-bfb010b612f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431387} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8251d7-0c1c-57d3-82c1-96e020e790ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431392} {:answer/id #uuid "7033358f-af7b-5515-ab10-9200cdcc75f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=0,\\,5,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431394} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d9cfdf-e3f3-5aa1-80a4-8f44ea2ba2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4d9cfdf-e3f3-5aa1-80a4-8f44ea2ba2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431426} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ddd1011-6a73-5b1e-91ae-c188d140fc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f41bc86-fed5-5013-8ded-0ffbd4097db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-14008" :question/problem "Solve the equation $6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3z.$ We can factor out the $3z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z & =0 \\\\ 3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{z}^{2}-7z-30$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0 \\\\ 3z(2z+5)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and $z=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "303a6c40-7dd3-5a1f-9ac2-fe9037107dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=0,\\,\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431376} {:answer/id #uuid "73b48f90-577a-5549-956b-b9cfd6428ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431395} {:answer/id #uuid "95cea456-477e-5349-9e24-76b0bd2aaa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{4}{3},\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431402} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4654cf-8099-5359-8263-b4873cfd74d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{2}{5},\\,0,\\,\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431405} {:answer/id #uuid "f921ee8e-f8e9-543a-8997-432bf272edf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f921ee8e-f8e9-543a-8997-432bf272edf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431432} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f973cd9-bf32-5279-9138-1e6ff7e9e31b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a08949f-c53d-51fb-8ad8-329357cd31bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37615" :question/problem "Find the solutions of the equation $20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}=6p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(p)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2} & =6p \\\\ 20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}-6p & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $p.$ We can factor out the $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{p}^{3}+7{p}^{2}-6p & =0 \\\\ p(20{p}^{2}+7p-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $20{p}^{2}+7p-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(20{p}^{2}+7p-6) & =0 \\\\ p(5p-2)(4p+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $p=0,$ $p=\\frac{2}{5},$ and $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b8f840-c042-5c1b-aae8-140a0d4c0a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,-\\frac{3}{2},\\,0,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431379} {:answer/id #uuid "39cefa24-96d5-5d54-83a4-f8e9b6c17839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,0,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431380} {:answer/id #uuid "65136a7e-c90f-5366-85b3-d50d34248626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431390} {:answer/id #uuid "894c9af7-3295-5667-8592-8ab4dbc017fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431399} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b0dcf6-1eae-5d92-92a7-6126d3337254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39cefa24-96d5-5d54-83a4-f8e9b6c17839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,0,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431380} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d173679-29f1-5f45-8ac9-08599341d0b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80b73972-1f36-59b0-b835-37011e29a2a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37650" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(x)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of ${x}^{2}.$ We can factor out the ${x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-7x-15) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{x}^{2}-7x-15$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-7x-15) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(2x+3)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1433db-adc1-52e3-b005-6ae48814f3e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-2,\\,-1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431367} {:answer/id #uuid "4c02e4f8-b708-5865-be16-cdfc2761fcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=0,\\,1,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431382} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c01589-5305-5536-b97c-c321ed32e67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,4,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431407} {:answer/id #uuid "af95cf1e-df56-56ac-995c-d2531f2040e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431411} {:answer/id #uuid "bb39f4bb-01e2-5f62-a94b-0ec48c3cca9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c02e4f8-b708-5865-be16-cdfc2761fcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=0,\\,1,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431382} :answer-field/id #uuid "6501bb07-1758-55b4-95cd-e544f548bb55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e59eba9b-7013-511c-87b7-74e803684799" :question/math-academy-id "q-37652" :question/problem "If $5{q}^{5}-7{q}^{4}+10{q}^{3}-6q=-{q}^{5}+11{q}^{4}-2{q}^{3}-6q,$ then what are the values of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(q)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{q}^{5}-7{q}^{4}+10{q}^{3}-6q & =-{q}^{5}+11{q}^{4}-2{q}^{3}-6q \\\\ 6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+10{q}^{3}-6q & =-2{q}^{3}-6q \\\\ 6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+12{q}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $6{q}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $6{q}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{q}^{5}-18{q}^{4}+12{q}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{q}^{3}({q}^{2}-3q+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${q}^{2}-3q+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{q}^{3}({q}^{2}-3q+2) & =0 \\\\ 6{q}^{3}(q-1)(q-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $q=0,$ $q=1,$ and $q=2.$\n\nTherefore, using the zero product rule, we have that the solutions are $0,\\,1$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0346dd7f-1ea8-565f-ae3a-2489bdd2b183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=0,\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431359} {:answer/id #uuid "761b3c80-100a-5ec0-b40d-e37f484f6891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=0,\\,\\frac{4}{3},\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431396} {:answer/id #uuid "a18ce679-6e85-500b-b3c5-b21e7df06b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=-\\frac{4}{3},\\,0,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431406} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1809a8-20e2-5b39-991b-3a166e873fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=-4,\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431409} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d57cb1-306c-5ebe-be17-8d75522b2940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=-2,\\,0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761b3c80-100a-5ec0-b40d-e37f484f6891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=0,\\,\\frac{4}{3},\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431396} :answer-field/id #uuid "1082cb6a-95fd-59f2-ab50-826ca5cf3578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "589b942b-2afa-544c-b0d4-95c41762ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-3981" :question/problem "If $15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}=-40r$, then $r=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(r)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2} & =-40r \\\\ 15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}+40r & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5r.$ We can factor out the $5r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{r}^{3}-50{r}^{2}+40r & =0 \\\\ 5r(3{r}^{2}-10r+8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3{r}^{2}-10r+8$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r(3{r}^{2}-10r+8) & =0 \\\\ 5r(3r-4)(r-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $r=0,$ $r=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $r=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a49e338-c372-5407-9df4-fad1f40d4c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\,2,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431363} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5e4519-cc30-5396-bd1c-9cef58c7772d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-6,\\,0,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431388} {:answer/id #uuid "84a02a9d-8984-54df-8d54-f34ac7891575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\,1,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431398} {:answer/id #uuid "e20eba27-efe7-579a-9a7f-eae6ba53a1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1,\\,0,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431425} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fb26bd-7ed2-5f2d-94d8-58300ffb1318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3,\\,-1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a5e4519-cc30-5396-bd1c-9cef58c7772d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-6,\\,0,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431388} :answer-field/id #uuid "7985aead-1b21-56ef-98f3-fd4a253086a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ace9a7c-7f26-5739-81ff-450b62fa7683" :question/math-academy-id "q-3985" :question/problem "Solve for $t$ where $-2{t}^{3}-10{t}^{2}+10t=-14t-2{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(t)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{t}^{3}-10{t}^{2}+10t & =-14t-2{t}^{2} \\\\ -2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+10t & =-14t \\\\ -2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+24t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $-2t.$ We can factor out the $-2t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+24t & =0 \\\\ -2t({t}^{2}+4t-12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${t}^{2}+4t-12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2t({t}^{2}+4t-12) & =0 \\\\ -2t(t+6)(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-6,$ and $t=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b0aa04-be1e-54a7-b230-b4434a74a514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2,\\,0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431370} {:answer/id #uuid "2b89dd01-b3fc-5526-9c13-3a06aab8a233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431371} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9dfe8f-8341-5528-919c-63a6c5c4a648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431391} {:answer/id #uuid "9972ffb9-d058-59a6-9b51-c552ecb84de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431403} {:answer/id #uuid "c084c6aa-2fc6-5f4f-b3a8-d6744f77733c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1,\\,0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b89dd01-b3fc-5526-9c13-3a06aab8a233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431371} :answer-field/id #uuid "d775bf5a-0368-53ae-878e-5cd650356f18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dba95b8-77df-52c1-b86b-bcff843030e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-70388" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(y)=10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-5y.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(y)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5y.$ We can factor out the $5y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y(2{y}^{2}+y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{y}^{2}+y-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y(2{y}^{2}+y-1) & =0 \\\\ 5y(2y-1)(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $y=-1$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d803a67-e01a-5dae-a6e3-d71ed0191880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\,2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431374} {:answer/id #uuid "37e96be1-0228-5e41-a422-f5ac375955a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-6,\\,-1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431378} {:answer/id #uuid "6c74bc5f-77fa-5808-8bfd-22468b03eb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-5,\\,-2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431393} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea1023-9d04-542b-8780-58eb7dba28c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\,1,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431408} {:answer/id #uuid "d11d3833-f957-59c7-a006-29a06f0b643a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2,\\,0,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d803a67-e01a-5dae-a6e3-d71ed0191880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0,\\,2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431374} :answer-field/id #uuid "014820dd-53d9-5997-9a0b-dbb6f0ddd6df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a86a1c0-6162-5d37-ac2b-51fa127af01b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70389" :question/problem "Solve for $t$ if $4{t}^{3}-20{t}^{2}+24t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4t.$ We can factor out the $4t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}-20{t}^{2}+24t & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-5t+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${t}^{2}-5t+6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t({t}^{2}-5t+6) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-2)(t-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0$, $t=2$ and $t=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cca6df7-9375-5547-918c-7f576ca1fd47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=0,\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431373} {:answer/id #uuid "33b8c262-de59-5b53-9770-39b84a4898a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431377} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13abfe-652c-5ae7-98ae-1daa422c3094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431389} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1e9a9b-eba2-52b1-bcc4-60d934c2e6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{6},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431400} {:answer/id #uuid "cea2e85c-f504-5caa-8597-6774360787af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f13abfe-652c-5ae7-98ae-1daa422c3094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431389} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee49f43f-642d-52e4-92e0-629fb560b5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d93d4254-999a-5b0a-8dfe-92904ca8400e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70467" :question/problem "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(z)=18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(z)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n18{z}^{4}+3{z}^{3}-3{z}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{z}^{2}.$ We can factor out the $3{z}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $6{z}^{2}+z-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}(6{z}^{2}+z-1) & =0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}(3z-1)(2z+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073745 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed80c47-53d9-5894-8530-36afb2d9d35c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,0,\\,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431375} {:answer/id #uuid "55ce39c3-a5c4-556f-8a9d-09a127539449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,0,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431385} {:answer/id #uuid "76c51cb6-b218-5331-a5d1-7802a6ee2866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,0,\\,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431397} {:answer/id #uuid "c8446242-0fc8-5e64-a859-345d430616a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,3,\\,4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431419} {:answer/id #uuid "cacccae7-e7f1-56e9-83c1-6a751d25d21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,-3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cacccae7-e7f1-56e9-83c1-6a751d25d21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,-3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431420} :answer-field/id #uuid "48adc87a-6d3d-5e14-b95f-10e72dbfc197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9f18fac-7576-5250-96fc-957f32166255" :question/math-academy-id "q-71776" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}+24x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}+7x+12$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}+7x+12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=-4,$ and $x=-3.$"}]}
